[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-17", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 17 — the empty answer cost 30x the full one; planner hint withheld for input-narrowed clauses, Datalog row 62 → 94 ops/s", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-17.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 17 - the empty answer cost 30x the full one: the planner's hint meant one thing and the executor read another\n\n> Seed: iteration 16's S5 - make the Datalog path pay what the index path\n> pays; the dumps named hash-join-rows. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2,\n> rung SF1 subset, origin :cross-host (simeon), mix IC02/07/08/09,\n> impl :datalog, 8 slots (reference 62.4 ops/s, iteration 16).\n\n## Generate\n\n  Before touching the engine, time the four Datalog reads in-process on\n  the SUT host with the official parameters, split by whether the answer\n  is empty (iteration 14 had seen an empty IC08 9x slower than a full one\n  on a smaller subset and filed it, unchased).\n\n    op    n   empty   empty-p50-ms   nonempty-p50-ms   plan-ms\n    ic02  12    7         2.5              8.1           2.0\n    ic07  12    0         -                1.7\n    ic08  12    7        61.1              2.2\n    ic09  12   12       163.7              -\n\n  Thread dumps of the IC08 loop: every busy sample inside\n  datalog.core/hash-join-rows -- the broad scan + group-by -- reached from\n  clause 0. The planner's plan for IC08 says clause 0\n  `[?msg \"hasCreator\" ?person]` :estimated-rows 0 for an empty person.\n\n  Cause, read off both sides: kotobase-peer.core/datalog-query-plan hands\n  datalog.core `:clause-cardinality` = the estimate AFTER `:in` inputs are\n  substituted (0 rows for this person); datalog.core's budget reads that\n  number as the size of the BROAD relation a hash join would scan and\n  takes `0 <= 4 x 1 group` as \"tiny relation, scan it whole\" -- 86,932\n  rows grouped to find nothing. A person WITH messages estimates above 4\n  and stays on the keyed path, which is why the full answer was cheap.\n\n  Prediction: withhold the hint for a clause an input narrows (the\n  executor's documented default is the keyed path) -> IC08 empty ~2 ms,\n  nothing else moves; served IC08 -> the rtt; throughput at 8 slots\n  90-100, IC09-bound.\n\n## Rank\n\n  First fix tried: estimate the broad relation for input-narrowed clauses.\n  Measured: ~10 ms per clause on that relation; IC08 non-empty 2.2 -> 13,\n  IC02 2.5 -> 7.4. REJECTED -- the cure cost more than the disease.\n\n  Second fix (landed): hint withheld for input-narrowed clauses.\n\n    in-process, 12 warm passes, p50 of 7 repeats:\n    ic08 empty 61.1 -> 2.0   nonempty 2.2 -> 2.1\n    ic02 2.5/8.1 -> 2.4/8.3   ic07 1.7 -> 1.5   ic09 163.7 -> 162.7\n\n    served, cross-host, 8 slots, impl datalog:\n    rate  on-time%  thru/s  ic02  ic07  ic08   ic09\n     64   100.00    63.3   14.7  12.2  12.5  213.5\n     96   100.00    93.9   15.3  13.7  14.5  293.1   <- highest passing\n    128    19.22    99.3\n\n  Prediction held on every point. Test added:\n  plan_cardinality_test/an-input-narrowed-clause-is-withheld-from-the-hint,\n  shown failing on the previous planner (2 assertions) and passing now;\n  suite 260 tests / 791 assertions / 0 failures.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - A 2-pass warm-up read the fix as 4x WORSE (IC08 non-empty 2.1 -> 9.9,\n    IC02 2.8 -> 8.9). Twelve passes and a p50 of seven repeats showed both\n    unchanged. Cold-JIT A/B numbers compare compilers, not code -- the\n    loop nearly recorded a refutation of a correct fix.\n  - The defect is a meaning mismatch across a seam, not a slow line: two\n    docstrings each say what THEIR number is, and neither says it is the\n    other's. The receipt records which meaning the executor needs.\n  - What is left on the Datalog path is IC09 alone, and it is all empty\n    answers: every official IC09 parameter carries a maxDate before this\n    subset's window, the two-hop join still materialises friend-of-friend\n    x messages (~164 ms), and the harness filters by date afterwards. The\n    engine already has `fuse-value-ranges` (a range clause from\n    `[?m date ?d] [(< ?d max)]`); the harness never asks for it.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: GROWTH on the Datalog column\n(62.4 -> 93.9 ops/s, rung/origin/mix/inflight/impl held). The index\ncolumn (255.6 / 511.1) is unaffected by this change.\n\nSeeds for iteration 18, in order:\n\n  - S7 (query shape): write IC02/IC09's date bound INTO the query\n    (`[(< ?date ?max)]`) so fuse-value-ranges turns the creationDate\n    clause into a range scan before the join, and move the top-20 to the\n    engine's :order-by/:limit. Predicted: IC09 empty -> ~5 ms (the range\n    is empty on this subset), non-empty near the index twin; Datalog row\n    toward the rtt ceiling (~250 at 8 slots). Gate: answers equal the\n    current Datalog and index paths on all 392 parameters.\n  - S6 (top-k): an ordered value index so the range scan is a cut, not a\n    filter of all 86,932 values.\n  - S2 (rung): full SF1 on simeon.\n  - S3 (mix): short reads IS1-IS7.\n"}]
